{
	title: 'Print mode: hal -p runs one prompt and prints the reply',
	spec: 'Like `claude -p`: `hal -p <prompt> [-d <dir>] [-m <model>]` (long forms --print, --dir, --model; options in any order; the prompt is the one argument that is not an option) opens a new tab in <dir> (default: the current directory; it must be an existing directory, else exit 2), switches its model as /model does when -m is given (aliases and :effort included), sends the prompt and waits for the turn to end. The turn is a full one, every tool included. The tab is an ordinary tab: every client sees it and the user may watch, steer, answer an approval or pause it. A completed turn prints its final text (what the model wrote after its last tool call) and a newline to stdout, exit 0. A refused model closes the tab again and, like a paused, failed or canceled turn or one blocked on a broken login, says why on stderr and exits 1. A question the turn asks is named on stderr (the turn waits for an answer in Hal). It joins the running host; if none answers (and no handover is pending), it hosts its own run in its process, without the web or recovery, and exits with it. It never leaves a process behind.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"connection.start resets pending commands, so print.run's begin() sends tab-new only after link.start resolves; earlier sends are silently lost (it hung). The /model reply and the prompt race: the prompt is submitted only after /model's output arrives, error or not, else a bad -m ran the prompt on the old model. Final text resets at each tool_call and drops the <summary> line (common/summary.ts). Hosting for its own run uses server.serve directly, skipping becomeHost (web, recovery, model refresh).",
		'Bug fix (session 15-eur): hal -p hung forever when -m named the current model, since 71z made /model print nothing then. Invariant: the prompt is sent after /model completes whether or not it prints. /model errors arrive just after its ack, so print sends a no-op complete command and submits on its ack; an error output first fails the run.',
		"Bug fix (session 50-del): hal -p hung forever when its turn hit a broken login (e.g. an auth.ason entry without accessToken or apiKey). By design (task j1) such a turn never ends: the host blocks it until the credentials file changes. Invariant: print fails on a non-question 'blocked' state of its turn, printing the preceding error output in full, exit 1, and closes its tab unless --keep or -s. Temporary errors still retry until they work (j1).",
		'Bug fix (session 50-dab): hal -p spawned a detached hal serve (a84084ba) that outlived the run, held the host lock and web port, and ran stale code with the run flags. Invariant: hal -p leaves no process behind.'
	],
	needs: ['cb']
}
